export const DATA_84: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-127", num: 127, title: "Đề 127", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Changing Role of Vietnamese Women in Modern Society", paragraphs: ["Vietnamese society has changed a great deal over the past few decades, and the position of women has changed along with it. In the past, most Vietnamese women stayed at home to take care of children and manage household chores while men were expected to earn money for the family. Today, this traditional pattern is no longer the only option. Vietnamese women now study, work, and make decisions in almost every part of public life. This shift has not happened overnight; it is the result of decades of gradual social and economic development across the whole country.", "One of the clearest signs of change is in education. Fifty years ago, many families believed that sending a daughter to school was less important than sending a son. Girls often left school early to help at home or to get married young. Nowadays, Vietnamese girls attend school at almost the same rate as boys, and in some universities, female students actually outnumber male students. Many young women now graduate with degrees in fields that used to be considered unsuitable for women, such as engineering, medicine, and information technology. This educational progress has opened doors that were once closed to them .", "Better education has led directly to wider job opportunities. Vietnamese women now work as doctors, teachers, engineers, bankers, and factory managers. According to recent labour reports, women make up roughly half of the country's total workforce, a figure that is high compared with many other countries in the region. A growing number of women have also started their own businesses, ranging from small family shops to larger companies that export goods abroad. These businesswomen are often praised for their hard work and their ability to balance strict deadlines with limited resources, and they are increasingly seen as important drivers of the national economy.", "Women's influence is also increasing in leadership positions, although progress here has been slower than in education or general employment. A number of Vietnamese women now hold senior posts in government ministries, state companies, and international organisations, and several provinces have elected female leaders in recent years. Even so, men still hold most of the very top positions in both politics and big business. Many analysts believe that old attitudes about who should give orders and who should follow them are the main reason for this gap, rather than any real difference in ability between men and women.", "Despite these achievements, many Vietnamese women continue to carry a heavy load at home. Even when a woman works full time outside the house, she is often still expected to do most of the cooking, cleaning, and childcare after she returns from work. This is sometimes called a double burden, because women effectively do two full-time jobs, one paid and one unpaid. Younger couples in cities are slowly beginning to share housework more evenly, with husbands taking on tasks that their fathers would never have considered doing, but this kind of sharing is still far more common in urban areas than in the countryside.", "Looking ahead, the role of women in Vietnam will likely keep evolving as education levels rise and government policies continue to support gender equality. New laws now guarantee women the same pay for the same work, and public campaigns actively encourage fathers to take a greater share of family duties. Still, closing the gap in pay and leadership completely will take more than laws alone; it will also require a genuine change in the way both men and women think about who does what at home and at work."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The reasons why Vietnamese women now attend university more than men.", "The complete disappearance of gender inequality in Vietnam.", "How the roles of Vietnamese women have expanded in education, work, and leadership, while some traditional burdens and inequalities remain.", "The struggles Vietnamese women still face at home, with occasional gains in education and work."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what has happened at some Vietnamese universities?", options: ["Male students now outnumber female students at every university.", "In some universities, female students outnumber male students.", "All female students choose to study engineering.", "Fewer girls attend school today than fifty years ago."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what proportion of Vietnam's workforce is made up of women?", options: ["More than two-thirds of all workers.", "Roughly half of the total workforce.", "Nearly all workers in the country.", "Less than one-third of the workforce."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do many analysts believe is the main reason men still hold most top leadership positions?", options: ["Women receive less education than men, limiting their opportunities.", "Traditional attitudes about who should lead and who should follow.", "Government laws forbid women from applying for senior posts.", "Women lack the necessary skills for leadership roles."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is a woman's situation at home sometimes called a \"double burden\"?", options: ["She must financially support two households.", "She works two separate paid jobs at the same time.", "Her husband completely refuses to help with any housework.", "She still performs unpaid housework and childcare on top of her paid job."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an occupation held by Vietnamese women?", options: ["Lawyers", "Engineers", "Bankers", "Factory managers"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"gradual\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["occasional", "step-by-step", "sudden", "constant"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"evolving\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["improving", "developing", "weakening", "repeating"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["universities", "families", "fields such as engineering, medicine, and IT", "Vietnamese women"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the pace of change in different areas of Vietnamese women's lives?", options: ["Progress in education and employment has occurred faster than progress in top leadership positions.", "Leadership roles for women changed before education became widely available to them.", "Employment opportunities for women have decreased despite educational progress.", "Educational progress led women to abandon paid work entirely."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Silk: A Fabric Woven Through History", paragraphs: ["Few fabrics carry as much history and prestige as silk. According to a well-known Chinese legend, it was Empress Leizu, wife of the legendary Yellow Emperor, who first noticed a silkworm cocoon and unraveled it into a single, continuous thread more than four thousand years ago. Whether or not this story is entirely accurate, archaeological evidence confirms that silk production was firmly established in China by around 2700 BCE. For centuries afterwards, the Chinese court guarded the secret of silk-making so closely that revealing it to foreigners was reportedly punishable by death.", "The production of silk begins with the silkworm, the larva of the domesticated silk moth, which feeds almost exclusively on mulberry leaves. After several weeks of continuous eating, the caterpillar spins a protective cocoon from a single filament of fibroin protein that can measure up to a kilometre in length. To harvest this thread intact, producers typically kill the pupa inside the cocoon with heat before the moth can emerge and break the strand. The cocoons are then boiled to loosen the sticky coating that binds the fibres together, allowing several filaments to be unwound and twisted into a single, wearable thread.", "Silk's economic significance grew enormously once trade routes connecting China to Central Asia, the Middle East, and eventually Europe became established. These routes, later named the Silk Road by a nineteenth-century German geographer, did not exist solely to transport fabric; spices, precious stones, and ideas travelled along the same paths. Nevertheless, silk remained so valuable that it was occasionally used as a form of currency, and merchants who controlled its trade could accumulate considerable wealth and political influence, tying the fortunes of distant empires to a fibre most of them had never seen produced.", "Silk's enduring appeal is not merely a matter of tradition; it also has genuine physical advantages. The triangular structure of its fibres reflects light at multiple angles, giving the fabric its distinctive shimmer, while the protein-based fibre is remarkably strong for its weight, comparable in tensile strength to some steel wires of the same thickness. Silk also absorbs moisture efficiently and adjusts to body temperature, making garments feel relatively cool in summer and warm in winter. These qualities help explain why, despite the rise of synthetic alternatives, silk has never been fully displaced from luxury clothing markets.", "Vietnam has its own long-standing relationship with silk production, dating back over a thousand years in certain northern regions. Villages such as Van Phuc, on the outskirts of Hanoi, and Hoi An in central Vietnam became renowned for weaving techniques passed down through generations of families. Vietnamese silk historically served both domestic ceremonial purposes, including traditional wedding garments, and export markets, particularly during periods of French colonial trade. Although mass production has since shifted much manufacturing to larger facilities, artisan workshops in these traditional villages continue to attract visitors interested in observing handloom weaving firsthand.", "Today, the global silk industry faces mounting pressure from cheaper synthetic fibres such as polyester and nylon, which can imitate silk's appearance without matching its production costs or labour intensity. Producing a single kilogram of raw silk can require several thousand cocoons and weeks of careful monitoring, a level of labour that synthetic manufacturing simply does not demand. In response, some producers have begun promoting so-called peace silk, a method that allows the moth to emerge naturally before the cocoon is processed, appealing to increasingly ethically-conscious consumers even though the resulting thread is generally shorter and less uniform.", "Whether prized for its shimmer, its history, or its increasingly ethical production methods, silk continues to occupy a distinctive place in both fashion and cultural memory, a fibre whose four-thousand-year story is still, in many respects, being written."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Silk has been completely replaced by synthetic fabrics in every market.", "The history, production, economic significance, and continuing relevance of silk, including its Vietnamese heritage and modern challenges.", "The technical process of producing silk and its modern industrial decline, with brief mention of its history.", "The legendary origins of silk in ancient China."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, approximately how long can a single silk filament measure, and what happens to the pupa in order to preserve it intact?", options: ["Several metres long; the moth is allowed to emerge naturally.", "Up to a kilometre long; the cocoon is boiled immediately without killing the pupa.", "Up to a kilometre long; the pupa is killed with heat before it can emerge.", "Up to ten kilometres long; the pupa is frozen to preserve the thread."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did the nineteenth-century German geographer do?", options: ["Discovered the techniques used to produce silk.", "Established the trade routes connecting China to Europe.", "First used silk as a form of currency.", "Named the trade routes connecting China to the West the \"Silk Road.\""], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which combination of properties explains silk's shimmer and strength?", options: ["Triangular fibre structure creates shimmer; moisture absorption creates strength.", "Temperature regulation creates shimmer; price creates strength.", "Triangular fibre structure creates shimmer; protein-based fibre strength is comparable to steel wire.", "Protein-based fibre creates shimmer; triangular structure creates strength."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what were two historical uses of Vietnamese silk?", options: ["Only religious temple decorations.", "Currency for trade along the Silk Road.", "Industrial machine parts and modern fashion runways.", "Ceremonial wedding garments and export during French colonial trade."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a reason for silk's continuing value?", options: ["Its historical prestige", "Its distinctive shimmer", "Ethical production methods like peace silk", "Its resistance to fire"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"displaced\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["discovered", "exported", "reduced", "replaced"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"mounting\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["overwhelming", "occasional", "traditional", "increasing"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"its\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["silk", "luxury clothing markets", "tradition", "synthetic alternatives"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Based on the passage, what can be inferred about the future of traditional silk-weaving villages like Van Phuc and Hoi An?", options: ["They are likely to disappear completely within the next decade.", "They will fully switch to synthetic fibre production to remain competitive.", "They may continue to survive by appealing to cultural interest and ethical, artisanal production rather than competing on price.", "They have already stopped producing silk for any commercial purpose."], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Academy Awards: Prestige, Politics, and the Persistence of an Institution", paragraphs: ["Since its inception in 1929, the Academy Award — colloquially known as the Oscar — has occupied a curious dual position in global culture: simultaneously the film industry's most coveted symbol of artistic achievement and a lightning rod for accusations that its judgments reflect commercial interest and insider politics as much as merit. [1] The statuette itself, a gold-plated figure standing just over thirteen inches tall, has become so recognisable that its silhouette functions almost as shorthand for cinematic legitimacy, regardless of whether audiences can name a single film nominated in a given year.", "The voting body responsible for conferring this legitimacy, the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences, has historically drawn criticism for its lack of demographic diversity. [2] Investigative reporting in the mid-2010s revealed that the voting membership skewed heavily toward older, white, male industry veterans, a composition that critics argued produced predictable patterns in which films and performers received recognition. In response to sustained public pressure, the Academy launched an initiative aimed at doubling the number of women and members from underrepresented communities within its ranks by a stated target year, a commitment that organisers described as ambitious rather than merely symbolic.", "Whether that initiative has meaningfully altered outcomes remains a matter of genuine scholarly disagreement. Some media researchers point to a modest increase in nominations for films directed by women and by people of colour as evidence of structural change, while others contend that headline nominations mask a persistent pattern in the technical and craft categories, where voting membership composition has shifted far more slowly. [3] The distinction matters because craft-category recognition — cinematography, editing, sound design — often determines which professionals gain access to future high-budget projects, meaning that surface-level progress in the marquee categories does not necessarily translate into broader industry transformation.", "The economic stakes surrounding the ceremony itself are considerable, though frequently misunderstood by casual observers. A Best Picture win does not, contrary to popular assumption, guarantee a dramatic box-office surge; the so-called Oscar bump has weakened substantially in an era where streaming platforms release films with minimal theatrical windows, denying audiences the extended re-release opportunities that once followed a win. What the award reliably does confer is negotiating leverage: directors and performers associated with winning films frequently command higher fees and greater creative control on subsequent projects, an effect that persists even when the commercial afterglow of the specific winning film proves negligible .", "Campaign spending for Oscar consideration has itself become a subject of controversy, with studios reportedly allocating tens of millions of dollars toward strategies once associated primarily with political elections — targeted screenings for voting members, sustained advertising in trade publications, and carefully orchestrated media appearances designed to keep a film's cast in public conversation throughout the voting period. [4] Detractors argue that this transforms the award from a pure assessment of craft into a contest partly determined by studio marketing budgets, effectively disadvantaging smaller, independently financed productions that lack comparable resources regardless of critical acclaim.", "Defenders of the institution counter that campaigning has always existed in some form and that voters, drawn from working professionals within the industry, remain capable of distinguishing genuine craftsmanship from manufactured buzz. They further note that several films with comparatively modest promotional budgets have secured major wins in recent years, suggesting that money alone cannot fully determine outcomes even if it demonstrably shapes the conversation surrounding contenders.", "Ultimately, the Academy Awards persist not because they represent an infallible measure of cinematic quality — few serious critics would claim such a thing — but because the institution has successfully maintained its symbolic authority across nearly a century of shifting technology, changing audience habits, and recurring controversy. That durability , more than any single year's outcome, may be the ceremony's most remarkable achievement."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The Oscars retain enduring symbolic authority despite persistent controversies over diversity, campaign spending, and questions about whether reform efforts have produced substantive change.", "The Academy's diversity initiative has fully resolved concerns about representation in voting outcomes.", "Campaign spending is the sole factor that determines which films win Academy Awards.", "The Academy Awards have lost all cultural relevance due to declining television ratings."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did investigative reporting in the mid-2010s reveal about the Academy's voting membership?", options: ["It had already achieved the diversity targets set by the initiative.", "It consisted primarily of members from outside the film industry.", "It skewed heavily toward older, white, male industry veterans.", "It was evenly split between younger and older members."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why has the \"Oscar bump\" weakened in recent years?", options: ["Ticket prices have risen too high for audiences to justify a return visit.", "Audiences have lost interest in Best Picture winners entirely.", "Studios have stopped campaigning for awards altogether.", "Streaming platforms offer minimal theatrical windows, removing the extended re-release opportunities that once followed a win."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what specific tactic do studios reportedly use during Oscar campaigns?", options: ["Withdrawing films from theatrical release to build exclusivity.", "Bribing individual Academy voters directly with cash payments.", "Filing formal complaints against competing studios.", "Targeted screenings for voting members and sustained advertising in trade publications."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why does the distinction between marquee categories and craft categories matter?", options: ["Craft-category recognition determines which professionals gain access to future high-budget projects, so progress in headline categories alone may not reflect broader industry change.", "Marquee category winners are chosen by a completely different voting body.", "Craft categories receive far more media coverage than marquee categories.", "Craft categories have already achieved full demographic parity."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an argument made by defenders of the Academy?", options: ["Films with modest promotional budgets have still won major awards.", "Campaigning has always existed in some form.", "All forms of promotional spending should be legally banned.", "Voters can distinguish genuine craftsmanship from manufactured buzz."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"negligible\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["insignificant", "predictable", "temporary", "controversial"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"durability\" in the final paragraph is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["popularity", "flexibility", "endurance", "profitability"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"that\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the voting membership composition", "the Academy's diversity initiative", "media researchers' disagreement", "the distinction between marquee and craft-category recognition"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This backlash played out publicly across entertainment journalism and social media, intensifying pressure on studios to justify their promotional strategies.'", options: ["[1]", "[3]", "[4]", "[2]"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Rise of Online Contraception Services and the Reshaping of Reproductive Healthcare Access", paragraphs: ["Over the past several years, a marked increase in the number of women turning to online clinics for contraceptive consultations and prescriptions has prompted researchers to examine what this shift may reveal about broader failures — or successes — within conventional healthcare systems. Telehealth platforms offering remote consultations, home-delivered prescriptions, and asynchronous messaging with clinicians have expanded rapidly, particularly in regions where in-person reproductive healthcare appointments often involve waiting periods measured in weeks rather than days.", "Proponents of this trend argue that it does not necessarily indicate a wholesale rejection of traditional medicine but rather a rational response to structural barriers that have long constrained access. Rural patients, for instance, may face journeys of considerable distance to reach a specialist, while patients in urban areas frequently encounter overbooked clinics and limited appointment availability outside standard working hours. Online platforms, by contrast, typically enable a consultation to be completed within minutes, with prescriptions often dispatched the same day — a convenience that may partially explain the trend's rapid uptake among younger demographics in particular.", "Critics, however, caution against interpreting this growth purely as a triumph of accessibility. Some clinicians have raised concerns that abbreviated online questionnaires may fail to capture medical histories with the same thoroughness as an in-person consultation, potentially overlooking contraindications relevant to certain contraceptive methods. This concern does not necessarily imply that telehealth consultations are inherently unsafe; rather, it suggests that the quality of screening protocols varies considerably across providers, and that regulatory oversight has not always kept pace with the sector's rapid commercial expansion.", "Privacy considerations further complicate the picture. For patients living in households where contraceptive use might provoke conflict — whether due to family disapproval, restrictive social norms, or concerns about a partner's reaction — the discretion afforded by an online consultation, conducted without a receptionist, a waiting room, or a physical prescription collected from a local pharmacy, may constitute not merely a convenience but a meaningful safeguard. Several studies examining patient motivations have found that discretion , rather than speed alone, ranks among the most frequently cited reasons for choosing a remote provider over a conventional clinic.", "The commercial dimension of this expansion arguably deserves closer scrutiny than it has generally received. Venture-backed telehealth companies operate under revenue models that may create incentives only loosely aligned with individual patient welfare; a platform whose profitability depends on consultation volume does not automatically face pressure to prioritise thorough screening over rapid throughput. This is not to suggest deliberate negligence on the part of such companies, but rather to note that market incentives and clinical best practice do not always converge without deliberate regulatory intervention.", "Public health researchers examining this trend have generally avoided framing it in binary terms of good versus bad, arguing instead that the growth of online contraceptive services reflects unmet demand that conventional systems have failed to adequately address, while simultaneously introducing new risks that existing regulatory frameworks were not designed to anticipate. Addressing the latter without dismantling the genuine accessibility gains of the former, several researchers argue, ought to be the primary policy objective going forward, rather than treating telehealth expansion and regulatory caution as inherently opposed goals.", "Whether healthcare systems will adapt quickly enough to integrate rigorous screening standards into an inherently faster-paced digital model remains an open question. What does seem reasonably clear, based on the consistency of findings across multiple studies, is that the underlying drivers of this shift — limited appointment availability, geographic barriers, and the desire for discretion — are unlikely to disappear on their own, meaning that the trend toward online contraceptive access is more probably a durable feature of the healthcare landscape than a temporary anomaly attributable to any single external circumstance."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Online contraceptive clinics are entirely safe and should replace traditional healthcare providers.", "Traditional in-person clinics remain the only reliable source of reproductive healthcare.", "Venture-backed telehealth companies deliberately prioritise profit over patient safety.", "The rise of online contraceptive services reflects genuine unmet demand rooted in structural barriers, while also raising screening and regulatory concerns that policy should address without eliminating accessibility gains."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what convenience do online platforms typically offer compared to traditional clinics?", options: ["Free consultations for all patients regardless of income.", "Guaranteed appointments with a specialist within the hour.", "Consultations completed within minutes, with prescriptions often dispatched the same day.", "Automatic enrollment in long-term prescription plans."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the concern about abbreviated online questionnaires actually suggest, according to the author?", options: ["That online questionnaires are more thorough than in-person interviews.", "That telehealth consultations are inherently unsafe and should be discontinued.", "That screening protocol quality varies across providers and regulatory oversight has not kept pace with commercial growth.", "That in-person consultations are now obsolete."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what reason do several studies identify as among the most frequently cited for choosing a remote provider?", options: ["Availability of a wider range of contraceptive brands.", "Discretion, rather than speed alone.", "Lower overall cost compared to traditional clinics.", "Recommendation from a family member."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the author suggest about the relationship between market incentives and clinical best practice?", options: ["They are completely unrelated to one another.", "They do not always converge without deliberate regulatory intervention.", "They always align naturally without any need for regulation.", "Market incentives are inherently superior to clinical best practice."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a reason patients choose online contraceptive services?", options: ["Speed of consultation and prescription delivery.", "Discretion in households where contraceptive use might provoke conflict.", "Lower cost compared to traditional clinics.", "Limited appointment availability at conventional clinics."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"discretion\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["efficiency", "privacy", "reliability", "affordability"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"scrutiny\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["regulation", "funding", "examination", "promotion"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["regulatory oversight", "the sector's commercial expansion", "the concern about abbreviated online questionnaires", "certain contraceptive methods"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Based on the final paragraph, what can be inferred about the future of the trend toward online contraceptive access?", options: ["It will be eliminated once stricter regulations are introduced.", "It is probably a durable feature of the healthcare landscape, since its underlying drivers are unlikely to vanish on their own.", "It will likely disappear once traditional clinics improve their appointment systems.", "It depends entirely on a single external circumstance that could change quickly."], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-187", num: 187, title: "Đề 187", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Wraight Course: Learning to Speak with Confidence", paragraphs: ["Ten years ago, a former high school teacher named James Wraight noticed that many of his adult friends felt nervous whenever they had to speak in front of a group, even a small one at work. He decided to design a short course to help people overcome this fear, and he called it simply the Wraight Course. What began as a handful of evening classes in a community hall has since grown into a program used by hundreds of local businesses, and Wraight himself now travels to different cities several times a year to teach it in person. Word of the course spread mainly through recommendations from past students rather than any advertising.", "The course itself lasts only six weeks, with one two-hour class each week, and it focuses almost entirely on practice rather than theory. In the first class, students simply introduce themselves in front of the group and are filmed doing so, a moment many describe as the hardest part of the whole course. Over the following weeks, they watch their own recordings, practice short talks on everyday topics, and slowly learn to notice habits such as speaking too quickly or avoiding eye contact. Wraight insists that reading a textbook about public speaking rarely helps as much as simply speaking in front of real people again and again.", "According to Wraight, the course works mainly because it removes the fear of doing something for the first time. By the third or fourth class, most students have already given several short talks, so standing up to speak no longer feels unfamiliar. He also believes that watching the video recordings helps students separate how nervous they feel from how nervous they actually look, since many students are surprised to discover that they appear far calmer on screen than they felt while speaking. Some evidence for this claim comes from surveys the course now asks every student to complete.", "Those surveys, collected after each class finishes, show that most students report feeling noticeably more confident by the final week, and many say the change surprised them. One recent graduate, a shop manager who had avoided speaking at staff meetings for years, said she volunteered to lead a meeting only two weeks after finishing the course. Not every student reports the same level of improvement, and Wraight admits the course works better for people who attend every session than for those who miss several classes, since much of the benefit comes from repeated practice rather than any single lesson.", "Today, several local companies pay for new employees to attend the Wraight Course as part of their training, and a few schools have asked Wraight to design a shorter version for teenagers. Wraight himself says he has no plans to turn the course into a large online program, since he believes the small class size and face-to-face practice are what make it effective. For now, at least, anyone who wants to take the course must still sign up for one of his evening classes in person, usually held in a rented room above a local library."], questions: [{ q: "According to the passage, how long does the Wraight Course last?", options: ["Six weeks, with one two-hour class each week", "Six weeks, with two-hour classes every day", "Six months, with one class every two weeks", "Ten weeks, with one two-hour class each week"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["James Wraight was a famous high school teacher before starting any course", "Every company in the region now requires new staff to take the course", "James Wraight created a practical course that has helped many nervous speakers", "Reading textbooks is more useful than any speaking practice course"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"overcome\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["remember", "avoid entirely", "get past", "become"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why did James Wraight originally decide to create the course?", options: ["He wanted to create an online business from home", "He noticed friends felt nervous speaking in front of groups at work", "He wanted to replace his job as a high school teacher", "He noticed his own students struggled with reading skills"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as part of the Wraight Course?", options: ["Completing a survey after each class finishes", "Watching recordings of your own short talks", "Being filmed while introducing yourself to the group", "Receiving a written textbook to study at home"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The phrase \"this claim\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the belief that most companies now pay for the course", "the belief that recordings show students looking calmer than they feel", "the belief that six-week courses work better than year-long courses", "the belief that reading textbooks is the best way to learn"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What did the shop manager mentioned in the passage do after finishing the course?", options: ["She opened her own version of the Wraight Course", "She immediately quit her job to teach public speaking", "She missed several classes but still finished the course", "She volunteered to lead a staff meeting two weeks later"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"noticeably\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["slightly", "rarely", "clearly", "nervously"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about why Wraight has not turned the course into a large online program?", options: ["He has already tried an online version and it failed completely", "He believes online courses always cost more to run than in-person ones", "He has been legally prevented from offering any online courses", "He values small, face-to-face classes for course effectiveness"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward the Wraight Course?", options: ["Enthusiastic, insisting every student should take the course immediately", "Strongly critical, warning readers the course rarely works", "Mildly positive, describing its methods and reported results factually", "Neutral to the point of expressing no information about outcomes"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Spread of Coffee: From Ethiopian Highlands to Global Habit", paragraphs: ["Coffee's origins are often traced to a folk tale involving an Ethiopian goat herder named Kaldi, who supposedly noticed his goats became unusually energetic after eating berries from a certain shrub. Whatever the truth behind this story, historians generally agree that the more reliably documented beginning of coffee drinking, as opposed to simply chewing coffee berries or leaves, took place several centuries later in the Yemeni region of Arabia, where Sufi monks reportedly used the drink to stay alert during long nights of prayer.", "By the fifteenth century, coffee cultivation and trade had become firmly established around the Yemeni port city of Mocha, and the drink soon spread northward into the wider Ottoman Empire. Coffeehouses, known in Arabic as qahveh khaneh, began appearing across cities such as Cairo, Damascus, and Constantinople, quickly becoming centers not merely for drinking coffee but for conversation, music, and political discussion, so much so that several rulers, wary of the debate these gatherings encouraged, periodically attempted to ban them outright, generally without lasting success.", "Coffee's arrival in Europe during the seventeenth century met with considerable suspicion at first, some of it religious in nature, since the drink was closely associated with the Ottoman world; one persistent, though probably apocryphal , story holds that Pope Clement VIII was asked to ban coffee as a 'Muslim drink' but instead tasted it, reportedly enjoyed it, and gave it his blessing instead. Whether or not this particular anecdote is accurate, coffeehouses spread rapidly through cities such as Venice, London, and Paris within a few decades, often functioning much as their Middle Eastern counterparts had.", "As demand grew across Europe, colonial powers began cultivating coffee far from its Ethiopian origins, establishing plantations in territories including Java, the Caribbean, and later Brazil, which would eventually become the world's largest coffee producer by a considerable margin. This expansion, however, relied heavily on enslaved and forced labor in many regions, a history that modern accounts of coffee's global rise increasingly acknowledge rather than omit, since the crop's spread cannot be fully separated from the economic systems that made large-scale cultivation profitable at the time.", "European coffeehouses developed their own distinct social role, particularly in London, where they became known as 'penny universities' because, for the price of a single cup, a visitor could join wide-ranging discussions on politics, science, and literature with strangers of varying social rank. Some historians argue that these spaces contributed meaningfully to the intellectual culture of the period, functioning as informal meeting points for merchants, scientists, and writers; others caution that their influence, while real, has likely been somewhat romanticized in later retellings of the era.", "By the twentieth century, coffee had completed its transformation from a regional Arabian drink into one of the most widely traded agricultural commodities in the world, its production, processing, and consumption spanning nearly every continent. This long and often uneven history — spanning religious controversy, colonial exploitation, and genuine cultural exchange — helps explain why coffee occupies such an unusually prominent place in daily life today, one that few other agricultural products, regardless of their nutritional importance, have managed to achieve on a comparable global scale."], questions: [{ q: "According to the passage, where does the more reliably documented history of coffee drinking, as opposed to folk tales, begin?", options: ["In the Ethiopian highlands, among goat herders", "In the Ottoman city of Constantinople, among traders", "In the Yemeni region of Arabia, among Sufi monks", "In the Italian city of Venice, among merchants"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"apocryphal\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["widely translated", "probably invented", "officially confirmed", "religiously forbidden"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Coffee expanded over time from Arabia to become a worldwide trade product", "Pope Clement VIII single-handedly made coffee acceptable throughout Europe", "Coffeehouses in London were more historically important than any other coffeehouses", "Coffee cultivation has always relied primarily on voluntary, well-paid labor"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how did some rulers respond to early coffeehouses in cities such as Cairo and Damascus?", options: ["They funded the construction of new coffeehouses citywide", "They occasionally tried to ban them but rarely achieved lasting results", "They required all coffeehouses to close permanently within a year", "They taxed coffee heavily but never attempted to ban it"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a location where colonial powers established coffee plantations?", options: ["Java", "Brazil", "the Caribbean", "Australia"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"their\" (in \"their influence, while real,\" paragraph 5) refers to _______.", options: ["the London coffeehouses", "the scientific discussions held there", "the merchants and writers who visited", "the historians studying them"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the passage say about Brazil regarding coffee production?", options: ["It grew to become the leading global producer of coffee by far", "It was the first territory where coffee plantations were ever established", "It produced coffee only for domestic consumption, not export", "It banned coffee cultivation until the twentieth century"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"romanticized\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["documented", "commercialized", "criticized", "idealized"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about how modern historical accounts of coffee's global spread differ from earlier ones?", options: ["They focus exclusively on Ethiopian folklore rather than any later history", "They are more inclined to address the impact of forced labor practices", "They argue that coffee spread only through peaceful trade with no exploitation involved", "They completely deny that colonial powers were involved in coffee cultivation"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's overall approach in presenting coffee's history?", options: ["Balanced, weighing both cultural significance and the darker aspects of its spread", "Dismissive, arguing coffee's cultural importance has been greatly exaggerated", "Purely celebratory, focusing only on coffee's positive cultural contributions", "Strictly chronological, listing dates without any interpretation or context"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "TikTok and the Adolescent Attention Economy", paragraphs: ["Few consumer technologies have generated as much anxious public commentary in as short a period as TikTok, the short-video platform whose algorithm-driven feed now occupies, according to some surveys, well over an hour of the average teenage user's day. Concern about the platform's effects on young people has coalesced around several distinct claims — that it shortens attention spans, that it worsens mental health, that it exposes minors to inappropriate content — each of which, on closer inspection, turns out to rest on a considerably thinner evidentiary foundation than the confidence of public debate would suggest.", "Attention span, perhaps the most frequently invoked concern, has proven particularly difficult to study rigorously, largely because researchers disagree substantially about what the term should even measure — sustained focus on a single task, the ability to resist distraction, or something closer to a subjective sense of restlessness. Studies comparing heavy short-video users to light users do find modest differences on certain laboratory attention tasks, but these differences are small enough, and confounded by enough pre-existing factors, that few researchers in the field are willing to claim the platform itself is the cause rather than merely a correlate.", "Mental health effects present an even murkier picture. Large-scale surveys do show a correlation between heavy social media use, TikTok included, and elevated rates of anxiety and depressive symptoms among teenagers, a finding replicated across enough studies that few researchers dispute the association itself. What remains genuinely contested is the direction of causality: some psychologists argue that heavy use worsens mental health directly, others that adolescents already struggling with anxiety or depression may simply be drawn toward heavy platform use as a coping mechanism, and a smaller group suggests both processes likely occur simultaneously, reinforcing each other over time in ways that current study designs struggle to disentangle .", "None of this is to say the platform carries no risk. Researchers who study adolescent development broadly agree that algorithmic feeds optimized purely for engagement can, for a meaningful minority of users, amplify content related to disordered eating, self-harm, or extreme political views far more efficiently than earlier social platforms did, since the recommendation system has no inherent mechanism for distinguishing engaging content from harmful content. This risk , several researchers emphasize, appears concentrated disproportionately among already-vulnerable teenagers rather than distributed evenly across the entire teenage user base.", "In response to these concerns, TikTok has introduced default screen-time limits for users under eighteen and expanded parental-control features, changes the company frames as evidence of responsible self-regulation. Independent researchers remain divided on how effective these measures actually are, since the limits are relatively easy for a determined teenager to bypass, and the platform's own data on how frequently users override the defaults has not been made available for independent verification, leaving outside assessments of the interventions' real-world impact largely speculative.", "Some researchers argue that comparing TikTok in isolation, rather than as one platform within a broader adolescent media environment that also includes messaging apps, gaming, and other video platforms, risks overstating its individual contribution to the mental health trends described earlier; a teenager's overall screen time across multiple platforms, this view holds, may matter considerably more than time spent on any single application. Whether this reframing meaningfully changes policy recommendations, or simply redistributes responsibility across a wider set of companies, remains disputed among researchers who study platform design.", "Taken together, the available evidence supports neither the alarmist narrative that TikTok is straightforwardly damaging an entire generation nor the dismissive view that concerns about the platform are merely moral panic detached from genuine risk. What it supports, more modestly, is a picture in which real, concentrated harms affect a meaningful minority of already-vulnerable users, while claims about broad, uniform damage to attention or mental health across the general teenage population remain considerably less settled than public conversation about the platform typically assumes."], questions: [{ q: "According to the passage, why has attention span been particularly difficult to study rigorously?", options: ["TikTok has refused to allow any independent researchers access to users", "No teenager has ever agreed to participate in an attention study", "Attention span cannot be measured using any laboratory task whatsoever", "Researchers disagree about what the term itself should measure"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"coalesced\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["come together", "dissolved", "disappeared", "multiplied"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["TikTok's screen-time limits have completely solved concerns about mental health", "TikTok poses no meaningful risk to any teenage user whatsoever", "TikTok has effects on teenagers that are far more mixed and complex than suggested", "TikTok has been proven to shorten the attention span of most teenagers"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what remains genuinely contested among researchers studying mental health and heavy platform use?", options: ["Whether teenagers use TikTok more than any other social platform", "The direction of causality between heavy use and worsening mental health", "Whether any correlation between heavy use and anxiety exists at all", "Whether depressive symptoms can be measured through surveys at all"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a factor discussed in relation to TikTok's effects on teenagers?", options: ["Amplification of harmful content toward an already-vulnerable minority of users", "The total number of TikTok employees dedicated to content moderation", "Disagreement over what attention span should measure", "Difficulty verifying how often teenagers bypass the platform's screen-time limits"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The phrase \"this risk\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["the possibility that screen-time limits are easy to bypass", "the exposure of vulnerable users to harmful content", "the disagreement over causality in mental health research", "the difficulty of measuring attention span in a laboratory"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do independent researchers remain divided on the effectiveness of TikTok's screen-time limits?", options: ["TikTok has refused to introduce any screen-time limits at all", "The limits have been proven completely ineffective by every published study", "The limits are relatively easy to bypass and usage data has not been made available", "Parental-control features are only available in a small number of countries"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"disentangle\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["strengthen", "separate out", "entertain", "simplify"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about comparing TikTok's effects in isolation from other platforms?", options: ["Doing so has been proven to be the only valid method for studying adolescent media use", "Doing so shows that TikTok causes more harm than every other platform combined", "Doing so may exaggerate TikTok's individual role in broader adolescent trends", "Doing so has led most researchers to recommend banning the platform entirely"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's overall purpose in the passage?", options: ["To criticize parents for failing to monitor their children's platform use", "To argue that TikTok's algorithm should be banned immediately by regulators", "To prove that concerns about TikTok are entirely unfounded moral panic", "To offer a balanced view that avoids extreme perspectives on TikTok"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Dyslexia Across Languages: Why Orthography Shapes the Disorder", paragraphs: ["Dyslexia, a neurodevelopmental condition characterized primarily by persistent difficulty in accurately and fluently decoding written words, was long assumed by much of the clinical literature to manifest in broadly similar ways regardless of the language in which a child was being taught to read. Not only has this assumption proven difficult to sustain under closer cross-linguistic scrutiny, but a growing body of comparative research now suggests that the specific reading difficulties associated with dyslexia — their severity, their detectability, and even the underlying cognitive profile most strongly implicated — vary considerably depending on how consistently a given writing system maps spoken sounds onto written symbols.", "Orthographic depth, the technical term for how transparently a language's spelling represents its pronunciation, turns out to matter enormously in this respect. Languages such as Italian and Finnish, whose spelling systems map sound to symbol with relatively few exceptions, are frequently described as 'shallow' orthographies, while English, whose spelling retains historical irregularities across thousands of common words, is considered comparatively 'deep.' Children learning to read in shallow orthographies typically achieve accurate decoding within the first year or two of formal instruction, a pace substantially faster than children learning English, where accurate decoding frequently continues developing well into later primary school years even among typically developing readers.", "This difference carries a significant, if somewhat counterintuitive, consequence for how dyslexia itself is identified and studied. In shallow orthographies, where most children achieve accurate decoding quickly regardless of underlying cognitive differences, dyslexia tends to manifest primarily as a persistent deficit in reading speed and fluency rather than accuracy, since even affected readers eventually learn to decode words correctly, just markedly more slowly than their peers. In deep orthographies such as English, by contrast, the same underlying cognitive deficit may instead manifest as persistent inaccuracy, since the irregular spelling system offers far more opportunity for decoding errors to persist uncorrected well beyond the point where a shallow-orthography reader would have resolved them.", "This orthographic-dependence does not mean dyslexia is somehow a different disorder in different languages, most researchers are careful to clarify; the underlying phonological processing deficit — broadly, difficulty manipulating the sound structure of language — appears substantially consistent across writing systems, and brain-imaging studies report broadly similar patterns of atypical activation in reading-related brain regions regardless of the language being read. What differs, this research suggests, is not the underlying deficit itself but how visibly and how quickly that deficit translates into a measurable reading problem within any particular orthography's demands.", "This distinction carries a practical implication that some clinicians consider underappreciated : a child being taught to read in a shallow orthography may show no obvious accuracy problems whatsoever, passing informal screening that focuses primarily on correct-versus-incorrect word reading, while nonetheless experiencing a genuine underlying phonological deficit that a fluency-focused or speed-based assessment would readily detect. Some researchers therefore argue that diagnostic criteria imported wholesale from deep-orthography research, particularly criteria weighted heavily toward accuracy errors, risk systematically underdiagnosing dyslexia in shallow-orthography populations, though this concern remains more strongly supported for certain shallow orthographies than for others, and comprehensive cross-national diagnostic data remain comparatively limited.", "Whether current screening tools used in shallow-orthography countries have been adequately recalibrated to detect fluency-based rather than accuracy-based deficits, of the kind described earlier, remains disputed among specialists in educational assessment; some argue that adequate recalibration has already occurred in several major shallow-orthography education systems, while others maintain that accuracy-oriented assumptions imported from English-language research continue to shape screening instruments more than orthographic differences would justify.", "None of this settles precisely how much of cross-national variation in reported dyslexia prevalence reflects genuine differences in underlying rates versus artifacts of orthography-mismatched diagnostic tools, a question current data cannot fully resolve. What the accumulated cross-linguistic evidence does suggest, cautiously, is that any account of dyslexia built exclusively around English-language reading development risks mistaking one orthography's particular symptom profile for a universal feature of the disorder itself, a risk that comparative research increasingly treats as more than merely theoretical."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Dyslexia's underlying deficit is consistent, but its manifestation depends on orthographic depth", "Dyslexia occurs only in languages with deep, irregular spelling systems such as English", "Brain-imaging studies have proven dyslexia is a completely different disorder in every language", "Shallow-orthography countries no longer need any dyslexia screening tools at all"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what distinguishes children learning to read in shallow orthographies from those learning English?", options: ["They achieve accurate decoding much sooner, usually within two years", "They achieve accurate decoding at exactly the same pace as English learners", "They never achieve accurate decoding regardless of how many years they study", "They achieve accurate decoding only after several years longer than English learners"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"underappreciated\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["financially undervalued", "not yet discovered", "widely publicized", "not fully recognized"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does dyslexia tend to manifest differently in shallow versus deep orthographies?", options: ["Only as a speech difficulty, never as a reading difficulty, in either orthography", "As lower fluency in shallow orthographies and persistent inaccuracy in deep ones", "As persistent inaccuracy in shallow orthographies but reduced fluency in deep ones", "Identically in both types of orthography, with no measurable difference"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a factor discussed in relation to dyslexia across languages?", options: ["Orthographic depth affecting how quickly children achieve accurate decoding", "The average age at which children begin formal reading instruction worldwide", "Consistent phonological deficits shown across languages in brain-imaging studies", "The risk of underdiagnosing dyslexia in shallow-orthography populations"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The phrase \"this concern\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["the risk of underdiagnosing dyslexia when using deep-orthography criteria in shallow ones", "the risk that screening tools have already been fully recalibrated everywhere", "the risk that brain-imaging studies cannot be replicated across languages", "the risk that English spelling will become even more irregular over time"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about a child in a shallow-orthography country who passes an accuracy-based reading screening?", options: ["The child will certainly develop reading difficulties later in a deep orthography", "The child may still have an underlying phonological deficit that a fluency-based assessment would detect", "The child's brain-imaging results would necessarily differ from those of an English-speaking child with dyslexia", "The child is guaranteed to have no phonological processing difficulties whatsoever"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"recalibrated\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["published", "calculated", "eliminated", "readjusted"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward claims that dyslexia manifests identically across all languages?", options: ["Fully convinced that dyslexia is an entirely different disorder in each language", "Skeptical, citing cross-linguistic evidence while affirming a shared core deficit", "Dismissive of any research on orthographic depth as unreliable", "Neutral, presenting no evaluation of the assumption at all"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do brain-imaging studies report about dyslexic readers across different languages?", options: ["Activation patterns that have never been studied in more than one language", "Completely different patterns of brain activation depending on orthographic depth", "No detectable brain activation differences between dyslexic and non-dyslexic readers in any language", "Similar patterns of atypical activation in reading-related brain regions across languages"], answer: 3 }] },
  ] },
];
